notes.model:
Single decision maker, two stages, discrete scenarios (Section 3, pp. 3-4).

Data. Set of physical item types $N$; printable items $N^p \subseteq N$. Knapsack weight capacity $W$ and volume capacity $V$ (the paper calls it a multidimensional knapsack; here two dimensions). Item $i$: weight $w_i$, volume $v_i$, reward $r_i$ if a physically brought item meets demand; printed reward $\alpha r_i$ with quality factor $\alpha\in[0,1]$ for $i\in N^p$. One unit of printing material: weight $w_b$, volume $v_b$. One 3D-printer: weight $w_p$, volume $v_p$. Printing item $i$ uses $m_i$ units of material and time $t_i$; each printer can print at most $T$ time units (models urgency, e.g. 24 or 48 h, p. 2). Scenarios $s\in S$ with probability $q_s\in[0,1]$, $\sum_s q_s=1$, and demand $d_i^s$. Printers are indexed by $P(x)=\{i\in\mathbb{N}^+ : i\le x\}$ for $x\in\mathbb{N}_0$.

First stage (decision vector $\mathbf a=((a_i)_{i\in N},a_p,a_b)$): $a_i$ copies of item $i$, $a_p$ printers, $a_b$ units of printing material.
TSS-3DKP: $\max \sum_{s\in S} q_s\,\mathcal{Q}(\mathbf a,s)$ s.t.
(1) $\sum_{i\in N} a_i w_i + a_p w_p + a_b w_b \le W$;
(2) $\sum_{i\in N} a_i v_i + a_p v_p + a_b v_b \le V$;
(3) $a_b \le a_p\,\mathcal{M}$ (big-$\mathcal M$: material only if at least one printer; the pdftotext layer drops the $\mathcal M$, the PDF shows it);
(4) $a_p,a_b,a_i\in\mathbb{N}_0$.

Second stage (scenario $s$, after demand is revealed): physically brought items that match demand are allocated, $a_i^s=\min\{a_i,d_i^s\}$, then the $a_b$ units of material are allocated to the $a_p$ printers; $p_{ij}^s$ = number of times item $i$ is printed on printer $j$.
$\mathcal{Q}(\mathbf a,s):=\max \sum_{i\in N} a_i^s r_i + \sum_{i\in N^p}\sum_{j\in P(a_p)} \alpha p_{ij}^s r_i$ s.t.
(5) $a_i^s\le d_i^s\ \forall i\in N\setminus N^p$;
(6) $a_i^s+\sum_{j\in P(a_p)} p_{ij}^s\le d_i^s\ \forall i\in N^p$;
(7) $a_i^s\le a_i\ \forall i\in N$;
(8) $\sum_{i\in N^p}\sum_{j\in P(a_p)} p_{ij}^s m_i\le a_b$;
(9) $\sum_{i\in N^p} p_{ij}^s t_i\le T\ \forall j\in P(a_p)$;
(10) $a_i^s\in\mathbb{N}_0\ \forall i\in N$; (11) $p_{ij}^s\in\mathbb{N}_0\ \forall i\in N^p, j\in P(a_p)$.
$a_i^s$ is a decision variable that, "due to our formulation", takes the value $\min\{a_i,d_i^s\}$ (p. 4). Key non-standard feature: the number of constraints (9) and variables (11) depends on the first-stage decision $a_p$ (pp. 4-5). Footnote 1 (p. 4): with $r_i=1$ for all $i$ the objective is the expected amount of satisfied demand.

Deterministic equivalent ILP-3DKP(Z) (Section 4.1, p. 5). Upper bound $Z=\min\{\lfloor W/w_p\rfloor,\lfloor V/v_p\rfloor,U\}$, where $U$ is the maximum over scenarios of the number of printers used when all demand $d^s$ is assigned to printers one by one in index order (all of item 1 first, then 2, ..., $|N^p|$, moving to the next printer when adding another item would exceed $T$). Binary $y_j=1$ if printer $j\in P(Z)$ is packed.
$\max \sum_{s} q_s\big[\sum_{i\in N} a_i^s r_i+\sum_{i\in N^p}\sum_{j\in P(Z)}\alpha p_{ij}^s r_i\big]$ s.t.
(12) $\sum_i a_i w_i + a_b w_b+\sum_{j\in P(Z)} y_j w_p\le W$; (13) same for volume;
(14) $a_b\le \sum_{j\in P(Z)} y_j M$;
(15) $a_i^s\le d_i^s\ \forall i\in N\setminus N^p,\forall s$; (16) $a_i^s+\sum_{j\in P(Z)}p_{ij}^s\le d_i^s\ \forall i\in N^p,\forall s$; (17) $a_i^s\le a_i\ \forall i,s$;
(18) $\sum_{i\in N^p}\sum_{j\in P(Z)} p_{ij}^s m_i\le a_b\ \forall s$;
(19) $\sum_{i\in N^p} p_{ij}^s t_i\le T y_j\ \forall j\in P(Z),\forall s$ (printing time becomes zero if printer not taken);
(20) $y_j\le y_{j-1}\ \forall j\in P(Z)\setminus\{1\}$ (symmetry breaking);
(21) $y_j\in\{0,1\}$; (22)-(25) integrality of $a_b,a_i,a_i^s,p_{ij}^s$.
The paper does not write $a_p$ explicitly in ILP-3DKP(Z); reading it as $a_p=\sum_j y_j$ is the natural (reader's) interpretation.

Assumptions: discrete scenarios (justified by the uniqueness of disasters, citing Grass and Fischer 2016, p. 3); one "ideal" printer type that can print all items with the same type of printing material and is easy to set up and use (stated as a limitation in Section 6); implicit in the model: one common quality factor $\alpha$, deterministic printing time and material use, printed items always reward $\alpha r_i$, and no time dimension beyond the per-printer budget $T$. The value of the big-M constant ($\mathcal M$ in (3), $M$ in (14)) is not given in the paper.
